(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["cs-12-07"] = {"1":{"s":"Advanced microprocessors have a wider word length (16, 32 and 64 bits), a larger address space, higher clock speeds and more features than the 8085, such as pipelining (doing the steps of several instructions at once), cache memory, memory protection and support for multitasking operating systems. They run the personal computers of today.","w":["The main differences from the 8085 are the wider data and address buses and the extra speed-up features.","Pipelining overlaps the fetching of the next instruction with the execution of the present one."],"x":["The 8085 is an 8-bit microprocessor with 64 KB of memory. Advanced microprocessors handle more bits at a time, address far more memory, run at much higher clock speeds and have extra features. They are used in personal computers, servers and many machines.","Features of advanced microprocessors: a wider word (16, 32, 64 bits); a large address space (megabytes and gigabytes); pipelining, in which the steps of several instructions are overlapped; cache memory, a small fast memory close to the CPU; memory management and protection; and support for multitasking and virtual memory.","They are made in families, and each new member can run the programs of the older members (upward compatibility)."],"e":"A pipelined processor fetches instruction 2 while it decodes instruction 1 and executes instruction 0, so it does about one instruction in each clock period."},"2":{"s":"The X-86 family of Intel started with the 8086 (16 bit) and went on with the 80286, 80386 (32 bit), 80486 and the Pentium processors. The 8086 has a 16-bit data bus, a 20-bit address bus (1 MB), a pipeline of a bus interface unit and an execution unit, and segmented memory. Each new member is upward compatible with the earlier ones.","w":["The 8086 has a 20-bit address bus, so it addresses 1 MB, not 64 KB.","The X-86 family is upward compatible: old programs still run."],"x":["The X-86 family is the series of Intel microprocessors that began with the 8086 and continued with the 80286, 80386, 80486 and the Pentium family. They are called X-86 because their names ended with 86.","The 8086 is a 16-bit processor. It has a 16-bit data bus and a 20-bit address bus, so it can address 1 MB of memory. It has two units that work at the same time: the bus interface unit, which fetches instructions, and the execution unit, which carries them out. This is a simple pipeline. Its memory is divided into segments.","Major attributes of the family: the word length grew from 16 to 32 and later 64 bits, the address space and the clock speed grew, and features such as cache, pipelines and protection were added. Each new processor can run the programs of the old ones."],"e":"A program written for the 8086 in the 1980s can still run on a modern X-86 processor, because the family is upward compatible."},"3":{"s":"The programming model of the 8086 has four 16-bit general registers AX, BX, CX and DX (each split into high and low bytes such as AH and AL), pointer and index registers (SP, BP, SI, DI), four segment registers (CS, DS, SS and ES), the instruction pointer (IP) and a flag register with nine flags. A 20-bit address is made from a segment register and an offset.","w":["AX is the accumulator; its two halves are AH and AL.","The 8086 flags are nine: six status flags (including carry, zero, sign, overflow) and three control flags."],"x":["The 8086 programmer sees these registers: four general purpose registers AX (accumulator), BX (base), CX (count) and DX (data), each of 16 bits that can be used as two 8-bit halves (AH and AL, BH and BL, and so on); the pointer registers SP (stack pointer) and BP (base pointer); the index registers SI (source) and DI (destination); and the instruction pointer IP.","The four segment registers are CS (code segment), DS (data segment), SS (stack segment) and ES (extra segment). Memory is divided into segments of up to 64 KB. A physical address of 20 bits is made by shifting the segment register left by 4 bits and adding the offset.","The flag register has nine flags: the status flags carry, parity, auxiliary carry, zero, sign and overflow, and the control flags trap, interrupt and direction."],"e":"If CS is 2000H and IP is 0100H, the physical address is 20000H plus 0100H, that is 20100H."}};
